WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.040
Philippine subsidies to preserve Ifugau rice terraces and install concrete canals instead sped

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:13.100
up their silting and decline. This is unintended consequences, episode 132. In the steep

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:18.560
valleys of Ifugau, farmers once cleared silt from narrow, hand-built channels that fed

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.280
the ancient pondered terraces in careful sequence. National programs arrived with payments for

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:29.200
terrace upkeep and money for rigid concrete irrigation lines meant to raise yields.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:34.440
The new channel's concentrated run-off so effectively that the old pond system began

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.600
filling with sediment faster than the terraces could be maintained.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:44.320
After the 1970s, Philippine National Program sought to protect the Ifugawa rice terraces,

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:50.480
recognized as both cultural heritage and productive farmland, while increasing food output for

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:51.680
a growing population.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:57.320
Officials reason that direct payments would keep farmers on the terraces, and that modern

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:02.020
concrete canals would deliver water more reliably than the traditional network of small,

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:08.620
adjustable ditches. At the time, both goals appeared compatible. Heritage preservation through

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:14.080
income support and agricultural modernization through better infrastructure seemed like a single

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:20.040
coherent approach. The programs reflected a broader policy view that combining cash incentives

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:24.720
with physical improvements, could stabilize rural landscapes and raise productivity

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:30.240
without displacing local communities. Decision makers worked from the information available,

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:36.260
visible terrorist deterioration and periodic water shortages, and treated the two interventions

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:42.340
as mutually reinforcing. They saw the stipends as a way to retain labor that might otherwise leave

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:48.260
for urban work. They saw the concrete as a durable fix that would reduce the hours spent rebuilding

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.600
building earth and banks after each monsoon.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:57.000
Programs rolled out across EFUGAO municipalities, offering maintenance stipends tied to terrace

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.480
upkeep and grants or loans for constructing concrete irrigation channels.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:07.400
Early reports noted higher rice yields in areas where the new canals reached fields consistently.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:13.360
Proponents highlighted the dual benefit of preserved cultural landscapes and improved harvests.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.760
Some local observers noted that the concrete structures altered the gradual, distributed

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.380
flow patterns long-managed by hand.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:27.080
Construction crews poured the channels along existing alignments first, then extended them

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:31.540
to steeper parcels that had previously relied on bamboo split pipe diversions.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:37.440
Once the forms were set, the fixed gradient and smooth interior walls produced higher flow

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.940
velocities than the rough, porous surfaces of the older ditches.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:46.480
Farmers accepted the stipends because they offset the cost of weeding and replanting that

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.060
had become harder as household sizes shrank.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:55.320
The rigid concrete canals concentrated water into fewer, faster moving paths, rather

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:59.500
than spreading it across the stepped pond system as the older channels had done.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:05.700
Run off therefore carried more soil into the ponds, accelerating siltation, and reducing

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.840
the terrace's ability to hold water through the growing season.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:14.860
As viability declined, many households shifted labor toward cash crop vegetables planted

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:18.240
on steeper slopes that required less pond irrigation.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:24.860
Those slopes, once left under secondary vegetation, now contributed additional sediment during

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:25.560
heavy rains.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.720
The maintenance payments, tied to visible terrace work, could not offset the loss of the underlying

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.060
water system that made the terrace's productive.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:39.820
Over successive seasons, the combined effects reduced the area that could be reliably planted

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:44.480
in traditional rice, even as subsidy records continued to show participation.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:50.600
One can trace the arithmetic directly, where a single adjustable bamboo gate once split

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:56.320
flow among three or four pawns in sequence, a single concrete outlet now delivered the entire

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.280
volume to the lowest pawn first.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:04.400
The upper ponds received overflow only after the lower one filled, so any excess carried

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:08.840
suspended particles straight through, rather than allowing gradual settling across the

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:09.480
full staircase.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:15.700
When silt accumulated faster than hand dredging could remove it, the water holding capacity

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:21.340
of each pond dropped, forcing farmers to shorten the rice growing cycle or abandon the lower

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:22.300
fields entirely.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.720
The stipend still arrived as long as the stone walls remained upright, yet the fields

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:33.340
them selves yielded less because the water arrived either too late or too muddy. The newly

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:38.560
cleared vegetable plots on the slopes above increased the sediment load entering the system at its

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:44.820
head, creating a feedback loop the original planners had not modeled. Once the siltation and labor

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:51.420
shift became evident, some programs experimented with hybrid designs that reintroduced smaller, adjustable

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:57.120
channels alongside the concrete lines. A number of terraces were later restored through community

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.960
led efforts that revived traditional flow management.

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:02.700
The current landscape shows a patchwork.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:07.800
Some lower terraces remain productive under mixed systems, while upper sections have

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.300
converted to vegetable plots or live follow.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:16.620
In several municipalities, engineers began leaving gaps in new concrete sections so that

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:20.060
farmers could still insert temporary wooden or bamboo checks.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.800
These adjustments reduced the rate of new siltation but could not recover ponds already filled

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.340
to the point where their retaining walls had to be raised, and added costs that fell

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:36.280
outside the original subsidy formulas. Incentive structures and physical infrastructure interact

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:42.480
with local practices in ways that simple addition rarely predicts. When a subsidy or built improvement

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:48.440
replaces rather than augments in existing management system, the original function can erode even

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:54.720
while the new payments or structures remain visible. The EFUGAO case suggests that mapping how water,

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.180
labor and sediment already moved through a landscape offers a useful check

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:04.120
before new channels or payments are introduced. How might similar programs today

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:08.100
test whether their infrastructure changes will reinforce or displace the

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:13.320
processes they aim to support? We'll leave it there for today. Thanks for thinking

